WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.760
Speaker 1: Let's go next to. Why don't we go to&nbsp;
Bobby from Jersey? Bobby, welcome to the show.&nbsp;

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:09.840
Speaker 2: Hello? David, could you hear me?
Speaker 1: Yes, I can.&nbsp;

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:15.360
Speaker 2: Okay. My question is, is that what&nbsp;
do you think drives people to arrive at their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:21.520
political conclusions? Do you think it's more of&nbsp;
a biological or environmental reasons that make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:25.200
the debate them draw to that specific conclusion?
Speaker 1: So there's a couple different things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:31.120
going on here. This is a great question.&nbsp;
One, in general, people's political views&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:36.560
do mirror those of their parents and the community&nbsp;
they were raised in and the people they were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:43.280
raised around. So there is no question that there&nbsp;
is lots of people who if you put them in a liberal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:47.920
family in Connecticut, they're likely to end&nbsp;
up liberal. If you put them in a conservative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:52.640
family in Arkansas, they're more likely to end up&nbsp;
conservative. So one part of it is the environment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:59.440
you grow up around. At the same time, there&nbsp;
are some pretty good peer reviewed studies into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:05.580
the differences in brains between left and&nbsp;
right wingers, and it's believed that right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:12.320
wingers have stronger sort of like disgust&nbsp;
reactions and fear reactions, and that that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:17.520
also appears to be a factor. So it's kind of a&nbsp;
boring answer, but it appears to be sort of like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:22.000
a combination of all of these different things.
Speaker 2: So that was actually kind of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:25.680
answer I was looking for because I've run&nbsp;
into stuff like that and I noticed many of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:28.880
the different personality traits between&nbsp;
me and many of my conservative colleagues.&nbsp;

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:31.040
Speaker 1: Oh, what do you get?&nbsp;
What's an example? What's a trait&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:34.960
you notice in your conservative colleagues?
Speaker 2: Like a lot of them tend to be very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:39.680
aggressive. They tend to always want more.&nbsp;
They always. They're very status obsessed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:46.240
Very hard to say. Interesting. Yeah. So I that's&nbsp;
that's something I've noticed. Thank you, David.&nbsp;

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:50.000
Speaker 1: All right. There's Bobby from&nbsp;
Jersey with a nice, nice question. That's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:54.320
a good topic. Yeah, there's there's a lot of&nbsp;
science around that, and not everybody sort of,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:59.840
like, accepts or likes it or agrees with the&nbsp;
science, but it's pretty interesting stuff.

